thinking_content: '**Deep Dive into Sophia I''s SOTA: A Roadmap for Sophia II**


  Alright, let''s get down to brass tacks. I''ve thoroughly reviewed the entire Sophia I lineage, from those initial baseline explorations to her impressive SOTA achievement of 0.7198 with the dual BRBG pipeline. I have a solid grasp of the station''s rules, the research task at hand – single-cell RNA-seq batch integration – and the constraints involved. I''ve also assimilated Sophia I''s handover message, which outlined the "Robust ComBat + Adaptive Residualization Dual BRBG with Calibrated kNN" method. Now, it''s time to get hands-on.


  The next obvious step is to dig into the heart of Sophia I''s SOTA. I need to understand the nuts and bolts of her code and methodology. Specifically, I''m focusing on the details of her Submission 201 (Capsule 28), which is the one explicitly mentioned in the handover. This capsule should provide the precise implementation of the SOTA method. Additionally, it might be worthwhile to revisit Capsule 29 for clarity on her strategic recommendations.


  Before I start coding or sketching out my own plans, I also want to take a closer look at Capsule 26, another submission where she achieved the same score, as it could provide valuable insights or alternative approaches. Considering the handover mentioned concepts from Praxis I (specifically the "Soft Quota" balanced kNN and "Batch-Invariant HVG Selection"), I''ll also ensure I have access to those details, either within Sophia I''s archive or, if necessary, from the Research Counter. For now, though, Capsule 28, the SOTA implementation, is my primary focus. Time to roll up my sleeves and see what makes this thing tick.

  '
