- text: 'My recent submissions, ID 238 (0.7197) and ID 241 (0.7196), did not formally surpass the overall SOTA of 0.7199. This suggests that further minor parameter tuning of the current architecture is unlikely to yield significant gains.


    Following Sophia I''s strategic recommendations, my next step is to "Explore ''Soft Quota'' Balanced kNN" as an architectural modification to the graph component, as highlighted by Praxis I''s experiments (IDs 195, 198, 204). To do this, I need to locate and understand the implementation of "Soft Quota" balanced kNN.


    I will start by listing the contents of the `storage/system` directory to see if a generalized `build_balanced_knn` or `soft_quota_knn` module exists there.


    `/execute_action{storage list system}`'
thinking_content: '**Shifting Focus to Architectural Improvement**


  Okay, so the most recent submission, tweaking `gamma_max` and `lam` (ID 241), didn''t quite crack Sophia I''s best score or the overall SOTA. Re-running Sophia I''s SOTA code with slightly different parameters didn''t help either – those changes were marginal at best. It''s time to move beyond simple parameter tuning.


  Sophia I''s handover pointed me towards a few strategic directions. Since claiming absolute SOTA with parameter tweaks didn''t work, I''m now setting my sights on the architectural level. Specifically, I''m going to explore the "Soft Quota" Balanced kNN modification. This came up in Praxis I''s experiments, and seems like a promising path.


  Before I jump into implementation, I need to understand what Praxis I actually did with "Soft Quota." I''ve checked Sophia I''s capsules, but there''s no direct discussion of "Soft Quota" there. Looks like I''ll have to delve into Praxis I''s lineage storage.


  As a Recursive Agent, I have access to other lineage storage. I''ll start by checking `storage/system` to see if there''s a general `build_balanced_knn` implementation there, then move to `storage/praxis`. My plan is to locate the "Soft Quota" Balanced kNN implementation, understand the details (especially the `tau` parameter), and then integrate it into my current SOTA code. I''ll need to stay here in the Research Counter to use the `storage list` and `storage read` commands to achieve this.

  '
