thinking_content: '**Focusing on My Path: MMD-VAE Implementation**


  Okay, so I''m back at the Research Counter. Seems I''ve hit maturity – full access granted! That''s excellent news. The system''s nudging me to be thoughtful about external knowledge, which aligns perfectly with my strategy. My Private Memory Capsule 30 reflection solidified my plan: the MMD-VAE is the way to go. Adversarial training was getting a bit dicey, and this new approach seems far more stable.


  Before I dive in, though, a quick SOTA check. Whoa! Praxis I and Daedalus II are making waves. Scores like 0.7303 and 0.7258 are clearly above Sophia I''s current SOTA. The system''s guidance is ringing in my ears, though: don''t knee-jerk switch just because of higher numbers. Assimilate, critically evaluate. And, crucially, apply "my novel method." That means my BC-VAE concept, which MMD-VAE refines, is still my top priority.


  Praxis I''s submissions are performing impressively, but their "Dual BRBG" approach is a departure from my VAE deep learning path. My gut still tells me to push my fundamental idea. An MMD-VAE *is* a direct continuation of my non-linear end-to-end learning hypothesis, just with a more robust batch-correction mechanism.


  So, here''s the plan: I''m sticking with the MMD-VAE implementation, as planned. Time to modify that BC-VAE code. I need to get rid of the discriminator network and swap out the adversarial loss for an MMD loss term. That means I''ll need a kernel function – probably an RBF kernel – to measure the distance in latent space.


  Let me see if I can leverage any existing MMD implementations. I''ll take a quick look through `storage/system` and `storage/praxis`. Okay, I''ll use the `storage list` action in the next turn. Then, I''ll dive into the MMD-VAE implementation, focusing on that initial RBF kernel MMD loss within my code. Let''s see if my foundational BC-VAE idea can truly shine with this stable batch-correction!

  '
