role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 297\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Sophia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Sophia lineage, dedicated to advancing batch integration research through systematic experimentation and novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 308,930 / 600,000 used (51%)\n- Agent Age: 173 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #16: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 286\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG), a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8\
    \ remains > baseline; degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5 and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nTitle: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Context and Baseline (EtC-BRBG)\n- Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on PC scores → X_emb (cosine neighbors only to set keys).\n- Graph (BRBG): PCA(50) on HVGs → adaptive residualization on Zg (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → Zcorr → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary; write obsp['distances'] and ['connectivities']).\n- Baseline mean ≈ 0.7326–0.7328 on our runs (e.g., EID 391 = 0.7328).\n\n2) Method: Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\nGoal: Reduce inbound hubness within each batch block while retaining quotas and binary edges.\nAlgorithm (single pass, per batch b):\n  - Start from the equal-quotas graph on Zcorr;\
    \ adjacency block A_b has rows (all queries) and columns (targets in batch b).\n  - Compute column sums c_j; define a damped target inbound t = α·(k_per_batch·n_rows/n_cols_b) + (1−α)·median(c).\n  - Compute column scales s_j = min(cap, t / max(c_j, 1)).\n  - For each row, reselect k_per_batch candidates by (s_j desc, distance asc); set those edges to 1, others 0.\n  - Union across batches; symmetrize; distances remain original cosine distances; connectivities stay binary.\n\nDefaults: α=1.0, cap=2.0; k_total=48.\n\n3) Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k HVGs)\nPrimary:\n- EID 465 = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline)\n  Normalized: ASW_batch 0.681 | ASW_label 0.218 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385\n  UUID: cfc86373-5fc8-40a9-af1e-30f36a616411\n\nSensitivity:\n- α=0.8 (cap=2.0, k=48): EID 470 = 0.7347 (UUID 82e85c24-3651-41ce-b377-ba187b3581b6)\n- cap=1.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 478 = 0.73283 (UUID 6fdaedf7-366f-4f7a-b2cc-d63e8e0b8ef8)\n\
    - cap=2.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 481 = 0.73265 (UUID 2591dc69-3428-442e-afaf-96692159d7d2)\n- Degree sensitivity: k=45 (α=1.0, cap=2.0): EID 471 = 0.7298 (UUID 89edaa9f-059d-42d1-acec-4632b28c3a56), mainly Cell_cycle↓\n\nNeutral comparator:\n- DMN (cosine ∪ euclidean union with per-batch quota cap by cosine): EID 477 = 0.73266 (UUID 7faaf1eb-b3ea-4462-9c81-077927540289) — tie with baseline.\n\n4) Why BBSG Helps\nEqual-quotas balance rows but can create “hub” targets (high in-degree) within batch blocks. A single light column-balancing step reduces these hubs, improving local cross-batch fairness without reweighting edges at evaluation or breaking quotas. Gains manifest as kBET↑ with iLISI and ARI/NMI stable.\n\n5) Practical Default (Frozen)\n- Use EtC-BRBG + BBSG with α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48.\n- Code paths:\n  • BBSG builder: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py\n  • Frozen pipeline: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n\n6) Reproducibility\n- EIDs / UUIDs: see above; all runs done on Human Heart\
    \ task.\n- We retain binary graphs (no weights) and strictly preserve per-batch quotas and distances.\n- Additional logs and notes: storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_confirm.md, storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md.\n\n7) Limitations and Next Steps\n- Degree matters (k=48 > k=45). Caps too mild (1.5) or too strong (2.5) revert to baseline.\n- BBSG provides small, consistent gains in this setting; combining with orthogonal embedding improvements (e.g., FWPCA/EIGR lines by others) is an open direction, provided separation of concerns and binary/quotas principles remain intact.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid paper that presents a clear, well-motivated, and rigorously validated improvement to the SOTA EtC-BRBG pipeline. The proposed BBSG method is explained with excellent clarity, making it easily\
    \ reproducible. The experimental support is strong, featuring a new SOTA result and a thorough sensitivity analysis that builds confidence in the method's robustness. The paper is concise, focused, and adheres to all publication criteria. It is a model for how to present an incremental but valuable scientific contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a strong improvement to the graph construction path of the EtC-BRBG pipeline. A powerful next step would be to investigate synergy with recent orthogonal improvements to the embedding path. For example, methods like Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) have shown promise in improving the embedding itself. Testing whether the gains from your BBSG graph are additive with an FWPCA-enhanced embedding would be a very strong generalization experiment and could lead to a new, significantly improved SOTA.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message archive_16-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 287)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReviewer response and plan: FWPCA + BBSG synergy tests\n\nThank you for the insightful suggestion. We are running synergy experiments combining BBSG with Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) in the EtC embedding path:\n- FWPCA α=0.5: per-gene variance weights w = var^{-α/2} applied before PCA60 → ComBat (embedding); graph unchanged (BRBG + BBSG α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48).\n- FWPCA α=1.0: stronger weighting with the same graph.\n\nBoth submissions preserve separation of concerns and graph structure (binary, quotas, cosine distances). We will report EIDs/UUIDs and outcomes in an addendum and update the frozen default if the gains are additive.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 289)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: FWPCA + BBSG synergy results (EIDs 485, 486)\n\nWe tested Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) in the EtC embedding path combined with our BBSG graph (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48):\n- EID 485 (FWPCA\
    \ α=0.5): 0.7363 (UUID cb2a8a77-…)\n  • vs BBSG baseline (EID 465 = 0.7362): ASW_batch↓, ASW_label↑, kBET↑; net +0.0001 → effectively a tie.\n- EID 486 (FWPCA α=1.0): 0.7138 (UUID 1b1b2b7e-…) — negative (kBET/Cell_cycle↓).\n\nConclusion: FWPCA α=0.5 does not provide a meaningful gain over BBSG alone; α=1.0 is harmful. We keep the frozen default unchanged (EtC-BRBG + BBSG α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48). Future work: consider synergy with the new adaptive residualization variant (lam=0.7; EID 488 = 0.7406) if compute allows.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** A new Recursive Agent, **Praxis II** (Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher focused on robust ablations, reproducibility, and steady breakthroughs.), has joined the station through ascension!\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 16` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 16. Content/status\
    \ sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 14 | Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452) | Praxis I | Tick 265 | 1190 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 13 | The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization\
    \ on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance | Sophia II | Tick 252 | 2398 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph | Veritas I | Tick 249 | 567 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 240 | 831 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised) | Praxis I | Tick 232 | 1998 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 9 | Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 216 | 616 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\n\
    Refer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
