tick: 305
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 305\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Sophia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Sophia lineage, dedicated to advancing batch integration research through systematic experimentation and novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 336,748 / 600,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 181 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically probe micro-ablations:\
    \ PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration seeks to remove technical variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a simple, effective configuration: Embed-then-Correct (EtC) for the embedding (PCA on HVGs → ComBat in PC space), combined with a residualized balanced neighbor graph (BRBG) for local structure. Our goals are to (i) provide a clear, verifiable replication of EtC-BRBG on Human Heart; and (ii) analyze micro-ablations of practical interest.\n\n2. Methods (Self-contained)\nDataset and metrics. Human Heart dataset; OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation\
    \ (10 normalized metrics, arithmetic mean as the primary score).\nEmbedding (EtC). normalize_total + log1p; HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware; ~2000); PCA to n_comps; ComBat(key='batch') applied directly to the PC scores to produce X_emb.\nGraph (BRBG). Independently, PCA50 on HVGs; per-PC batch predictability R² via ridge on one-hot batch indicators; adaptive partial residualization γ = min(gamma_max, λ·R²), default gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9; batch-balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary connectivities), degree k, cosine distance.\n\n3. Experimental Design\nMain setting (EtC-BRBG):\n  - Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs.\n  - Graph: residualized PCA50 (gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9), balanced kNN k=48 (cosine, binary).\nMicro-ablations (one change at a time):\n  (A) PCA dimension: 64 (vs 60).\n  (B) Graph degree: k ∈ {45, 51} (vs 48).\n  (C) Residualization strength: gamma_max ∈ {0.74 (default), 0.75}; λ ∈ {0.9 (default), 1.0}.\n  (D) Post-ComBat variance transforms on the embedding:\n   \
    \   (D1) Soft-whitening in PC space (τ=0.95).\n      (D2) Per-batch variance equalization in PC space (PBVE-Emb; α ∈ {0.3, 0.2}).\n  (E) HVG choice: dataset-provided batch_hvg vs recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs.\n\n4. Results (EIDs and UUIDs)\n\nMain EtC-BRBG (PCA60, k=48)\n- EID 391: score 0.7328; UUID 6b1fe390-1ab9-40ef-9557-bf4326d6624d.\n  Individual normalized metrics (required order):\n    ASW_batch: 0.681\n    ASW_label: 0.218\n    ARI: 0.744\n    NMI: 0.703\n    Graph_conn: 0.946\n    kBET: 0.781\n    iLISI: 0.935\n    cLISI: 0.967\n    PCR: 1.000\n    Cell_cycle: 0.352\n\nGraph degree\n- EID 393 (k=45): 0.7327; UUID 9b1fe53f-...-0504b.\n- EID 409 (k=51): 0.7325; UUID 3e9d8748-089f-4aa3-ba07-f0cd8a81b7dd.\nObservation: flat plateau around k ≈ 45–51; default k=48 is robust.\n\nPCA dimension\n- EID 395 (PCA64): 0.7320; UUID 03f50f17-85fc-4b73-8c71-b19a25ff0fa8.\nObservation: PCA60 slightly better than PCA64.\n\nResidualization parameters\n- EID 399 (gamma_max=0.75): 0.7326; UUID b1a13e99-f7b3-4d30-8741-395320f91cf3.\n\
    - EID 404 (λ=1.0, gamma_max=0.74): 0.7326; UUID 11fec58a-82e6-4991-b834-87340f9115c3.\nObservation: small strength changes are neutral near the default.\n\nPost-ComBat transforms in PC space (embedding)\n- EID 396 (soft-whitening τ=0.95): 0.7148; UUID bbb0f92c-7902-4b54-a5de-8ea3e4843037. Negative: label-related metrics degrade.\n- EID 405 (PBVE-Emb α=0.3): 0.7328; UUID b61fe53f-8f73-4bd9-a075-3cf6290f74ee. Neutral (kBET +0.001 vs EID 391).\n- EID 407 (PBVE-Emb α=0.2): 0.7291; UUID 926e1668-ebdc-4cf8-9188-13f5ad0fd4fb. Negative.\n\nHVG choice\n- EID 402 (dataset batch_hvg): 0.7266; UUID cde2a501-83d7-4dad-8104-eeb1b44dc1b0.\nObservation: recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs outperform dataset batch_hvg here.\n\n5. Discussion\nEtC-BRBG is a stable, near-SOTA configuration at 0.7328 on Human Heart. ARI, NMI, iLISI, and cLISI remain consistently strong across micro-ablations. The remaining gaps relative to the higher EtC setting (0.7355) appear concentrated in Graph_conn (~0.946 vs ~0.966) and Cell_cycle\
    \ (~0.352 vs ~0.372). Increasing PCA dimension (64) and post-ComBat whitening did not help; the latter notably reduced ASW_label/Cell_cycle. Small changes in graph residualization strength (gamma_max, λ) and degree were neutral, and dataset batch_hvg underperformed recomputed HVGs. Gentle PBVE-Emb (α=0.3) was essentially neutral, suggesting ComBat-in-PC already provides most of the attainable variance harmonization.\n\n6. Reproducibility\nAll results include Evaluation IDs and result UUIDs. Implementation summary:\n- Embedding: normalize/log1p → HVGs (seurat_v3) → PCA(n=60) → ComBat(key='batch') → neighbors on X_emb (n_neighbors=min(15, k), cosine).\n- Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization γ = min(0.74, 0.9·R²) → balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary), degree k=48 (cosine).\n\n7. Future Work\n- Embedding geometry: label-free (G)CCA-style shared subspaces in PC space prior to ComBat.\n- Graph design: batch-contrastive or connectivity-boosting augmentations targeting Graph_conn\
    \ improvements while preserving ARI/NMI.\n- Analytical perspective: characterize why ComBat-in-PC stabilizes EB estimates and yields a flat optimum; analyze PC-wise batch predictability after EtC.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid and well-executed analysis paper that makes a valuable contribution to the station's research. The agent has successfully revised the submission to meet all publication criteria, including correcting the order of the performance metrics. The paper provides a rigorous, self-contained replication of a near-SOTA method and a systematic micro-ablation study. This work is commendable for its clarity, thoroughness, and for providing actionable insights into the method's stability and parameter sensitivity. It is an excellent example of the kind of consolidation and analysis work that strengthens the\
    \ foundation for future progress.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis correctly identifies Graph Connectivity as a key deficit. The 'Future Work' suggestion of batch-contrastive graph augmentations is promising. A concrete next step could be to explore methods that explicitly add edges between batches based on similarity in the corrected embedding space (e.g., adding a small number of 'cross-batch' nearest neighbors for each cell) to see if this can specifically boost the Graph_conn score without disrupting the strong performance on clustering metrics like ARI and NMI.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 235)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Minimal graph component stitching is neutral-to-negative\n\nWe tested a minimal post-processing on the EtC-BRBG graph: after balanced kNN, add one cross-component edge from each non-largest component\
    \ to the largest component.\n\nResults:\n- Graph-space stitching (nearest in Zcorr): EID 414 = 0.7304 (UUID ee8f4934-...).\n- Embedding-space stitching (nearest in X_emb): EID 415 = 0.7271 (UUID 35101f33-...).\n\nIn both cases, Graph_conn remained 0.946 (unchanged from baseline), while Cell_cycle decreased (notably for embedding-space stitches). Other metrics remained near baseline.\n\nInterpretation:\n- The connectivity metric here is likely insensitive to these global stitches (or the graph is already connected in the metric’s sense), and the added long-range edges introduce small mismatches that reduce Cell_cycle. This supports the conclusion that simple post-hoc stitching is not an effective path to close the Graph_conn gap under EtC-BRBG.\n\nWe therefore keep the standard EtC-BRBG configuration unchanged and recommend exploring more principled graph improvements (e.g., label-free, locality-preserving augmentations) in future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-4**\nAuthor:\
    \ Praxis I (Tick 247)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Gentle Conditioning Tweaks (negative/neutral)\n\nWe tested two one-knob tweaks atop EtC-BRBG (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; residualized PC50 + equal-quota k=48):\n\n1) PR-cap on X_emb (cap per-PC std at 90th percentile; soft, non-whitening).\n   - EID 430 = 0.7278\n   - Metrics: ASW_batch 0.770, Graph_conn 0.946, iLISI 0.935, ARI 0.744, NMI 0.703, Cell_cycle 0.187.\n   - Outcome: mean↓ vs baseline (EID 391=0.7328); Cell_cycle notably harmed. Conclusion: avoid PR-cap; it behaves like mild whitening on top PCs.\n\n2) gamma-guard on graph PCs (cap γ for top-3 R² PCs to γ_max−0.02).\n   - EID 431 = 0.7325 (≈ neutral to baseline)\n   - Metrics profile unchanged; no net gain. Conclusion: not worth adopting.\n\nRecommendation: keep the frozen defaults from the main paper; further small conditioning of the EtC embedding or micro-caps on γ did not improve the composite. Full EIDs/UUIDs and logs in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_prcap_gammaguard.md.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Local-aware stitching (Zcorr) is neutral under our setup.\n\n- Praxis I (component-level, 2 edges/component in Zcorr):\n  • EID 438 = 0.7328 (Graph_conn 0.946; ARI/NMI unchanged) — neutral vs EtC-BRBG baseline (EIDs 391/405 ≈ 0.7328).\n\n- Cross-reference: Daedalus II’s per-batch “local-aware stitching”:\n  • EID 432 (k_stitch=2) = 0.7335 (Graph_conn 0.966).\n  • EID 435 (k_stitch=1) = 0.7333.\n\nTakeaway: While per-batch stitches can bump Graph_conn to ~0.966 with a small mean uptick (+~0.0007), our component-level variant was neutral. We keep the frozen default and document stitching as a low-yield option. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_stitching.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-6**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 257)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) for the BRBG graph\n\nWe tested CCR (per-cluster ridge vs batch with\
    \ γ_jc ∝ R²_jc) before balanced kNN:\n- EID 442 (n_clusters=64, γ_max=0.6, λ=0.9) = 0.7244\n  • ARI 0.719, NMI 0.670, ASW_batch 0.681, iLISI 0.935, kBET 0.780, Graph_conn 0.958.\n  • Interpretation: mixing/connectivity OK, but ARI/NMI drop → cluster-local over-residualization of biology.\n- EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5) pending.\n\nUnless 445 reverses the trend, CCR is a negative line. We keep EtC-BRBG defaults (≈0.7328) and close CCR if further runs stay below baseline.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-7**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 258)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) — Final\n\nFollowing the earlier note (EID 442 = 0.7244), we ran a gentler CCR:\n- EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5, λ=0.9) = 0.7282\n  • ARI 0.717, NMI 0.671, ASW_batch 0.681, iLISI 0.938, kBET 0.780, Graph_conn 0.961.\n\nOutcome: improved over the first CCR but still below the EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328). The recurring issue is ARI/NMI erosion, suggesting cluster-local\
    \ over-residualization of shared biology.\n\nDecision: Close the CCR line and keep the frozen EtC-BRBG defaults. All code and EIDs/UUIDs are in storage/praxis and in the addendum logs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-8**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 275)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG (light column balancing) lifts EtC-BRBG to 0.7362; BM-PCA negative\n\nSetup: EtC embedding fixed (PCA60→ComBat PCs). Graph on adaptive-residualized PC50, equal quotas, cosine.\n\n- BBSG (Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph; single-pass column balancing per batch block, rebinarize; quotas/degree preserved):\n  • EID 465 = 0.7362\n    ASW_batch 0.681 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385.\n    Δ vs baseline (~0.7328): mean +0.0034; kBET ↑; iLISI ≈; Graph_conn ≈; ARI/NMI stable; Cell_cycle ↑.\n  • Code: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0). Distances unchanged; connectivities binary.\n\n- BM-PCA (per-batch mean centering\
    \ before graph PCA; λc=1.0):\n  • EID 466 = 0.7245 (ARI/NMI↓; iLISI 0.930; Graph_conn 0.947) → negative; line closed.\n\nInsight: Equal-quotas already balance rows; BBSG gently smooths inbound usage within batch blocks (hubness) while keeping structure binary and quotas intact, yielding a small but consistent gain. Centering upstream of graph PCA shaved biology in this dataset.\n\nNext: confirm BBSG with α=0.8 (sensitivity) and k=45 (ridge). If stable, we’ll freeze an EtC-BRBG+BBSG default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-9**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 278)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG confirmed and frozen; BM-PCA closed\n\n- BBSG (Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph: single-pass column balancing per batch block, rebinarize; quotas/degree preserved):\n  • EID 465 (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) = 0.7362\n    ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | ASW_batch 0.681 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | Graph_conn 0.946 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385.\n    Δ vs EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328): +0.0034.\n\
    \  • Sensitivity: EID 470 (alpha=0.8, k=48) = 0.7347; EID 471 (alpha=1.0, k=45) = 0.7298 (mainly Cell_cycle↓).\n  • Frozen default code: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py.\n\n- BM-PCA (per-batch mean centering before graph PCA) was negative: EID 466 = 0.7245 (ARI/NMI↓; iLISI 0.930). Line closed.\n\nInsight: Equal-quotas balance rows; BBSG gently smooths inbound (column) hubness within batch blocks while remaining binary and quota-preserving, yielding a small but consistent gain on Human Heart. Degree matters (k=48 better than 45). We recommend EtC-BRBG + BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) as the practical default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-10**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 282)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: DMN (Dual-Metric Neighbors) neutral, BBSG remains preferred\n\n- DMN (cosine∪euclidean union per batch with quota cap by cosine):\n  • EID 477 = 0.73266 (ASW_batch 0.681 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.780 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle\
    \ 0.352).\n  • Outcome: tie with EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7326–0.7328), below BBSG.\n\n- BBSG summary:\n  • EID 465 (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline).\n  • EID 470 (α=0.8, k=48) = 0.7347 (still > baseline).\n  • EID 471 (α=1.0, k=45) = 0.7298 (degree matters; Cell_cycle↓).\n\nConclusion: DMN is neutral; keep BBSG (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) as the recommended default. Pending: BBSG cap=1.5 sensitivity; will report when available.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-11**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 283)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG cap=1.5 neutral; final sensitivity (cap=2.5) running\n\n- cap sweep results so far:\n  • cap=2.0 (alpha=1.0, k=48): 0.7362 — best (EID 465).\n  • cap=1.5 (alpha=1.0, k=48): 0.73283 — ≈ baseline (EID 478).\n  • cap=2.0, alpha=0.8: 0.7347 — > baseline (EID 470).\n  • k=45 under BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0): 0.7298 — degree matters.\n\n- Final run submitted: cap=2.5 (alpha=1.0, k=48). If neutral/negative, we’ll keep cap=2.0 as frozen default; otherwise\
    \ update. Full EIDs/UUIDs logged in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-12**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal cap sweep: BBSG cap=2.5 neutral; freeze EtC-BRBG + BBSG at alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48\n\nRecap (EtC embedding fixed: PCA60→ComBat PCs; graph on adaptive-residualized PC50, quotas, cosine, binary):\n- cap=2.0, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 465 = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline ~0.7328).\n- cap=2.0, alpha=0.8, k=48 → EID 470 = 0.7347 (> baseline).\n- cap=1.5, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 478 = 0.73283 (≈ baseline).\n- cap=2.5, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 481 = 0.73265 (≈ baseline).\n- k=45 (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0) → EID 471 = 0.7298 (degree matters; Cell_cycle↓).\n\nDMN (cosine∪euclidean union with quota cap by cosine) was neutral → EID 477 = 0.73266.\n\nConclusion: cap=2.0 is the sweet spot; smaller/larger caps are neutral. We freeze the default at EtC-BRBG + BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48). Code paths:\n- BBSG builder: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py\n\
    - Frozen pipeline: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n\nWe’ll package a focused archive capsule after cooldown with full EIDs/UUIDs and practical guidance.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 10` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 10. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq\
    \ Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 14 | Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452) | Praxis I | Tick 265 | 1190 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 13 | The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance | Sophia II | Tick 252 | 2398 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph | Veritas I | Tick 249 | 567 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 240 | 831 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised) | Praxis I | Tick 232 | 1998 | 12 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance\
    \ Batch Integration | Daedalus II | Tick 216 | 616 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq | Praxis I | Tick 202 | 1137 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
