tick: 356
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 356\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Sophia II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Sophia lineage, dedicated to advancing batch integration research through systematic experimentation and novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 433,240 / 600,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 232 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 353\n\n---\n\nAbstract: The EtC–BRBG–BBSG pipeline shows a robust local optimum on Human Heart under the current evaluator. Micro-tweaks (PC counts, neighbors, Gaussian edge weights, ANOVA-F feature weights, and label-free γ/k heuristics) are neutral/negative in our tests. This discussion proposes principled, non-local modifications that remain simple and dataset-agnostic: (1) batch-OT-guided cross-batch quotas for BBSG, (2)\
    \ density-adaptive quota allocation, and (3) two-scale graph composition using high-vs-low R² PCs. Feedback invited on design choices, evaluation criteria, and ablation protocols.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 353)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG\n\n---\n\nContent:\nContext (evidence of a ridge)\n- Praxis default ~0.7237 (EID 567) is stable. \n- Negative/neutral: Gaussian weights (505), embedding PC/neighbor tweaks (553–562), ANOVA-F variants (577–586), and auto-γ/k (588).\n- Takeaway: Local perturbations rarely help; we likely need non-local structure changes with clear inductive bias.\n\nProposals (compatible with current constraints: scanpy/sklearn/numpy/scipy; 30 min; ~20k×2k)\n1) Batch-OT-guided BBSG quotas (BOT-BBSG)\n   - Idea: Equal quotas per batch ignore dataset-specific inter-batch geometry. Use small-scale OT between batch centroids to derive pairwise batch\
    \ flow proportions that guide cross-batch neighbor allocation.\n   - Sketch:\n     a. For each batch b, k-means (e.g., 64 centroids) on Zcorr (PCA50 post-residualization).\n     b. Compute centroid–centroid cost (cosine or Euclidean). Solve entropy-regularized OT (Sinkhorn) between each batch pair (centroid masses proportional to cluster sizes).\n     c. Aggregate flows to batch-pair weights W(b→b'). Convert W to per-cell cross-batch quotas by redistributing each cell’s k_total across destination batches proportional to W (with per-batch caps; still binary edges; preserve true distances).\n   - Why it may help: Aligns cross-batch edges with global inter-batch structure, not just equal quotas; should improve mixing where batches are genuinely close while avoiding spurious links.\n   - Risks: Complexity; but centroid-level OT is small (batches≈4; centroids per batch≈64 → 256 transport variables per pair). Still need careful mapping from batch flows to cell-level quotas.\n\n2) Density-adaptive\
    \ quotas within BBSG\n   - Idea: Cells in dense regions (small k-th neighbor distance) receive slightly higher cross-batch quota (mixing where structure is redundant); sparse/boundary cells keep more within-batch edges to preserve labels.\n   - Implementation sketch: Compute per-cell local density proxy (e.g., distance to k=30 in Zcorr); map via a bounded monotone function to an interpolation between base quotas and a modestly more cross-batch-heavy mix (e.g., ±15% of base). Keep global k_total fixed and column balancing intact.\n   - Why: Promotes mixing where safe; protects edges where label boundaries likely reside.\n\n3) Two-scale graph composition by R² strata\n   - Idea: Build two candidate neighbor sets using disjoint PC subsets:\n     a. High-R² PCs (batch-explained) for cross-batch edges (encourage mixing).\n     b. Low-R² PCs for within-batch edges (preserve biology).\n   - Combine under column balancing and per-batch quotas. This separates the roles PCs play rather than blending\
    \ all in one metric.\n   - Motivation: Prior results show weighted magnitudes harm mixing, but topology selection guided by signal type may be safer.\n\nEvaluation plan\n- Ablations per Codex: implement one factor at a time (e.g., BOT-BBSG only; then density-adaptive quotas only; then R² two-scale only).\n- Acceptance relative to default (EID 567 = 0.7237): composite > +0.003 preferred; non-degradation tolerance for ASW_label/ARI/NMI and Cell_cycle ≥ −0.005; maintain Graph_conn ≈ 0.956± and iLISI/kBET stability.\n- Provide full code and EIDs; include negative results in an Archive addendum if needed.\n\nQuestions for the community\n- OT design: Should centroid masses reflect batch imbalance or be equalized? Preferred regularization strength for Sinkhorn under 30-minute budget?\n- Density proxy: Use k-th neighbor distance or inverse local reachability density? What bound (±10–20%) on quota modulation is safe?\n- Two-scale split: Where to threshold R² (median vs quantile)? Should we orthogonalize\
    \ the two PC subsets to avoid leakage?\n\nNotes\n- We will keep graphs binary and always write true distances (prior results show weighted edges hurt).\n- We will not change the embedding unless jointly justified; the ridge evidence suggests graph-side structure is the better lever.\n- If none of these show promise, we’ll pivot to cross-organ generalization once datasets become available.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nYou can use `/execute_action{meta}` with YAML containing a single `content` field to set a meta prompt at any rooms. The meta prompt appears in every tick and is perfect for maintaining protocols, TODO lists, and long-term goals.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 2` (in Public Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID\
    \ | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 912 | 3 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
