thinking_content: "**Ready to Build the First ALNB Experiment**\n\nOkay, I've just finished prioritizing \"Adaptive Local Neighborhood Blending\" (ALNB) for robust graph construction as my next big research push, and I'm feeling focused. The plan is clear: start with my best FWPCA-enhanced EtC embedding (ID 475, score 0.7371) as the foundation. This will be the backbone for my initial ALNB experiment.\n\nThe first step is to replicate the FWPCA embedding from ID 475. I've got to ensure I have the right code. I know the FWPCA implementation is associated with submission ID 475, and I published Archive ID 17 that had the paper detailing the FWPCA method.  The code itself is what I need to retrieve and then adapt.  \n\nBefore diving into modifications, I need to check something I reviewed earlier, `graph_bbsg.py` from Praxis II (which is at `storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py`). I'm going to read that code first, to make sure I'm using the latest version of Praxis's BBSG.\n\nOnce I've verified the\
  \ code for ID 475, I'll move on to building the foundational version of ALNB. This involves generating multiple local neighborhoods, and then blending them. For this first experiment, I'll use a straightforward approach:\n\n1.  Start with the FWPCA-enhanced embedding from ID 475, using the alpha=1.0 configuration, which achieved the personal SOTA of 0.7371.\n2.  Generate two kNN graphs: a standard cosine kNN and a batch-balanced kNN (BBSG, likely simplified). I might directly integrate Praxis's BBSG if it's simple enough.\n3.  Implement a basic, non-adaptive blending module, simply averaging the connectivity matrices of the two kNNs. This \"Averaged Local Neighborhood Blending\" will be my initial Exp 1.0 for ALNB.\n\nThis will serve as a starting point for exploring the core concept of combining different neighborhood definitions and allow me to benchmark it against my FWPCA baseline (ID 475) and Daedalus III's SCA v3 baseline (ID 589). It's a simplified version of the full ALNB idea\
  \ but it'll allow me to test out the core idea.\n\nSo, I need to verify I have the correct version of code for ID 475, which I believe I do since I explicitly refer to it in tick 280 (personal SOTA) and tick 295 (the paper). I need to check the code.\n\nOkay, let's start with Praxis II's `graph_bbsg.py`, found in the expected location: `/execute_action{storage read storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py}`.  After this, I'm ready to dig into ID 475's code and get this first experiment going!\n"
